                <pb facs="0713" n="685" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v22/pg685"/>

                <head>410. Memorandum of a Telephone Conversation Between the Secretary of State and the Deputy Secretary of Defense (Robertson), Washington, October 1,
                    1956, 7:09 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d410-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-head" calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1956-10-01T19:09:00-04:00"
                            >October 1, 1956, 7:09 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">TELEPHONE CALL FROM REUBEN
                        ROBERTSON</p>
                <p>R. said Bendetsen came back<anchor n="2" xml:id="d410-f2"/> and there
                    have been several sessions with a large meeting today with all the military. It
                    strikes R. from what he has put together
                    so far there are 4 issues we may not be able to resolve happily in the
                        military.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d410-f3"/>
                    R. asked re the Sec’s seeing B. and also B.’s talking with the Pres. so he can reflect to Magsaysay the Pres. is familiar with the
                    problems—if we can get a position. If we can’t resolve them we may want him to
                    see things on the jurisdiction questions. The Sec. said he thinks before things
                    bust the Pres. should have a chance to express his views. The Navy and Radford, said R., probably will be the toughest hurdle. The lawyers are coming
                    along. The Sec. will call him in the a.m. to see him Wednesday. If we work
                    something out that seems satisfactory, it will be helpful to his prestige to
                    have him see the Pres., said the Sec.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d410-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d410-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Eisenhower
                    Library, Dulles Papers, General Telephone Conversations.
                    Transcribed by Phyllis D. Bernau.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d410-f2" n="2" xml:id="d410-f2-content">
                <p>Bendetsen returned to Washington for
                    consultations on October 1.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d410-f3" n="3" xml:id="d410-f3-content">
                <p>Apparent reference to the questions of criminal jurisdiction, acquisition of
                    additional land, customs controls, and the duration of the agreement.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
